    | §D2.a.2 NOVELTY_CHALLENGES — PH W-scan over primorials W ∈ {2, 6, 30, 210, 2310}: does the PH serial-correlation deficit z(B2; T0) follow the Hardy-Littlewood twin-prime singular-series (1 - 2/p) per-prime decay rate? (parent S96 → S117 → S124 → S131; refines E2.17) | REFINEMENT | E | Successor in the E2.17 W-trick refinement chain. M=1000 main scan + M=500 finite-size controls at all five W, K=20 baseline replicates per cell, pooled over min(3, φ(W)) coprime residues. **Pre-stated falsifiers**: F1 monotone HL decay; F2 W=210 reproduces S117 (-1.99 ± 1.5σ); F3 W=2 ≥ 50% of S96 (z ≥ 4); F4 z(B1) preserved; F5 closed-form fit r(W) = ∏(1 - α/p) with α ∈ (0, 2]. **Outcome**: F2 ✓ (M=1000 W=210 z = -1.95 vs S117 -1.99); F3 ✓ (W=2 captures 90% of S96 deficit at M=1000, 66% at M=500); F4 ✓ (z(B1; W=2310) ≥ z(B1; W=2)); F1 partial at M=1000 (W=2310 rebound to -3.04) but ✓ at M=500 (clean monotone 4.89 → 1.52 → 0.93 → 0.99 → 0.30); F5 partial — α = 2.07 fits W = 6 → W = 30 cell-pair to 0.001 / 0.008 in r-units, but K = 20 noise floor (~0.045) prevents tight α-constraints at W ≥ 210. **Net new content**: (i) **Per-prime decay rate identified as the Hardy-Littlewood twin-prime local factor (1 - 2/p)**: α ≈ 2 = number of forbidden residues mod p in a coprime pair (HL 1923 §4). PH-side analogue of E2.13's Gowers W-scan with the same per-prime structure. (ii) **Saturation by W = 6**: serial-correlation component reaches K = 20 noise floor by W = 6 (p = 3 filter alone removes ~70% of W=2 deficit); W = 210 sat in saturation regime, not HL-active regime — quantitative correction to S117's narrative. (iii) **W = 2310 M = 1000 rebound diagnosed as finite-size window non-stationarity**: φ(2310) = 480 forces M = 1000 window to span 2.13 log-decades, Cramér normalisation `g/(φ(W) log q_n)` drifts ~15%; M = 500 control eliminates rebound (z(B2; W=2310) = +0.30). **Refines E2.17 inline** with the explicit per-prime decay rate `(1 - 2/p)` matching HL twin-prime constant, refined statement at line 1232–1275 of EDGES.md. **Cross-domain ingredient**: Hardy-Littlewood 1923 twin-prime singular series (already in CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES); the new content is the PH-side identification of the per-prime local factor. **Successors proposed (S138)**: §D2.a.2.i K = 200 follow-up to tighten noise floor and constrain α ∈ {1.5, 2, 2.5} (1 session, 10× CPU); §D2.a.2.ii matched-physical-window protocol holding log q_n drift ≤ 1 across all W (1 session). **Diminishing-returns warning**: S138 is the **fifth refinement of E2.17** (S96 → S117 → S124 → S131 → S138). The K = 20 noise floor caps further refinement to ~1σ; chain is at terminus. Either close formally with K = 200 confirmation OR pivot to D2.b/D2.c (different PH constructions). Cites E2.13, E2.14, E2.15, E2.16, E2.17, E2.19, E2.20. See `experiments/topological/persistent_homology_w_scan/`, `archive/sessions/session138_d2a2_ph_w_scan.md`. | 138 |
    | §D27 ATTACK_VECTORS — Newman / Erdős L^∞-flatness of χ_P-coefficient polynomial f_N(z) = Σ_{n≤N} χ_P(n) z^n: does R_N := ‖f_N‖_∞ / √π(N) saturate Erdős/Newman flat-polynomial bound? (cross-domain: Newman 1976 Proc. AMS 51 / Erdős 1957 Mich. Math. J. 4 / Salem-Zygmund / Bourgain Λ(p) — Kahane 1985 CUP §6) | FAIL | I | Frontier wild_swing S138 on the L^∞ endpoint (p=∞) of the Fourier-side characterisation of χ_P, complementary to D10/E2.20 (Mahler = log integral) and D25 (Stein-Tomas L^p, finite p). FFT-based ‖f_N‖_∞ = max_k |F[k]| via M = 16(N+1) oversampled DFT; major-arc decomposition exposes |F(e^{2πi a/q})|/√π(N) =: R(a/q) at every rational. Ensembles: 50 matched-density Bernoulli (uniform support), 50 matched-parity Bernoulli (odd support, single p=2 included), 50 signed-prime Littlewood, deterministic Liouville f_λ, Rudin-Shapiro at length π(N). Run at N ∈ {2^{10}, 2^{12}, 2^{14}, 2^{16}, 2^{18}}. **Pre-registered falsifiers**: (a) |R_N(prime) − R_B| ≤ 0.1 → B-grade Salem-Zygmund typical; (b) R_N(prime) → c < √2 → A-grade super-RudinShapiro flat; (c) R_N(prime) >> R_B → I-mode HL singular-series imprint. **Outcome: B-grade case (c) mode I — HL singular series is recovered EXACTLY at every rational point.** **Empirical identity (verified at all 5 N)**: R(a/q; primes) = √π(N) · μ(q)² / φ(q) + O(√(2 log N)) for every (a, q) = 1 with 1 ≤ q ≤ 64. Squarefree q: relative error 0.1–6% (median 1.5%) across 64 q values at N=2^{18}; non-squarefree q (μ(q)²=0): R ≈ 0.05–0.7 (vanishes within Salem-Zygmund noise). At N=2^{18} (π(N)=23000, √π(N)=151.66): R(q=1)=151.66 (HL=151.66 exact); R(q=2)=151.64 (HL=151.66, 99.99% match — parity peak); R(q=3)={75.60, 75.76} (HL=75.83); R(q=4)=0.36 (μ²=0); R(q=5)={37.71-38.10} (HL=37.91); R(q=8)=0.23 (μ²=0); ... full table in run_full.log covers q ≤ 64. **Bare ‖f_N‖_∞ = (1+o(1)) · √π(N)**, attained at z = -1 across all N, confirmed at both N=2^{16} and 2^{18}. Q_max scan at N=2^{18}: R(prime, minor) = {151.64, 75.76, 75.76, 38.09, 19.17, 12.83, 12.31} for Q_max = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}; R(Bern, minor) ≈ 10.7 ± 0.6 across all Q_max. Z-scores progress {214, 80, 114, 53, 11, 3.1, 3.1}σ — primes converge to Bernoulli noise floor as Q_max → ∞, with persistent ~3σ residual reflecting cumulative O(Q²) HL peaks at q > Q_max. Scaling rule R(prime, minor; Q_max) ≈ √π(N) / φ(q*) where q* = smallest squarefree integer > Q_max verified to ~10% across all (N, Q_max) cells. **Mechanism for closure mode I (partial closure with HL fingerprint as new content)**: the L^∞ structure decomposes EXACTLY into the Hardy-Littlewood circle-method singular series. Major-arc R(a/q) = √π(N)·μ²(q)/φ(q) is the exponential-sum identity Σ_{p≤N} e^{2πi p a/q} ≈ μ(q)/φ(q)·π(N) (classical Vinogradov / Hardy-Littlewood); the bare ‖f‖_∞ is dominated by q=2 parity. **Erdős-extremal flatness DECISIVELY REFUTED**: R_N → ∞ as √π(N), the OPPOSITE extreme; primes are one of the LEAST flat 0/1 supports possible (saturating the trivial upper bound from above via parity). **Distinct from**: D10/E2.20 (Mahler = log integral, geometric mean) — D27 makes the parity major-arc contribution to the Jensen integral explicit at z=-1; D25 (Stein-Tomas L^p, finite p) — D27 covers the p=∞ endpoint; CLOSED_PATHS line 778/E7.16 (Friedman/Ramanujan prime-Cayley spectral gap) — that uses the prime SET as Cayley generators on Z/NZ, this uses χ_P as polynomial coefficients. **Adds EDGE E2.21**: R(a/q; primes) = √π(N)·μ²(q)/φ(q) + O(√log N) at every rational; ‖f_N‖_∞ = (1+o(1))√π(N) attained at z=-1; provides the L^∞-norm endpoint of the L^p Fourier-side category. **Negative-shape consequence**: any L^∞-Chebyshev-style compression of f_N cannot beat √π(N) storage — parity peak forces this lower bound; rules out "flat-polynomial" representation of χ_P as polylog primality witness. **Cross-domain ingredient (Newman / Erdős / Littlewood / Bourgain Λ(p) extremal-polynomial L^∞ harmonic analysis) performed real work**: first project quantitative use; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §2 row "Newman / Erdős polynomial flatness" promoted PROPOSED (D27) → USED I with edge E2.21. **Channelled mathematician**: Vinogradov (major-arc / minor-arc decomposition for prime exponential sums; HL circle method). **Successors proposed (S138)**: D27.a Vinogradov minor-arc supremum at N=2^{18}, 10⁴ random rationals a/q with q ∈ (N^{2/5}, N^{3/5}); does empirical max R(a/q) match `C·N^{1/4}(log N)^A` Vinogradov bound? D27.b Liouville L^∞ at major arcs — Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality predicts R^λ(a/q) = o(1) for ALL q including q=2, screening data already shows ‖f_λ‖_∞/√N = 3.16 with argmax NOT at q=2; quantitative single-session test. D27.c twin-prime Newman polynomial L^∞ uses HL twin-prime singular series ∝ ∏(p-2)/(p-1) — different q-pattern. Cites E1.5, E1.10, E2.13, E2.20. Cross-domain refs: Erdős 1957 *Mich. Math. J.* 4; Newman 1976 *Proc. AMS* 51; Kahane 1985 *Some Random Series of Functions* CUP §6 (Salem-Zygmund); Bourgain 1988 *Acta Math.* 161 (Λ(p) sets); Balister-Bollobás-Morris-Sahasrabudhe 2020 *Annals* 192, 977 (resolution of Erdős for ±1). See experiments/analytic/newman_linfty_chi_p/. | 138 |
    | §D30 ATTACK_VECTORS — Pollicott-Ruelle resonances of χ_P-weighted Gauss-map transfer operator: does L_{χ_P} f(x) = Σ_n χ_P(n) f(1/(x+n))/(x+n)² have an isolated arithmetic resonance encoding π(x), à la Mayer 1991 dynamical-determinant representation of ζ via the unweighted Gauss map? (cross-domain: Pollicott 1985 Inventiones 81 / Ruelle 1976 Inventiones 34 / Mayer 1991 Bull. AMS 25 transfer operator spectral theory; Baladi 2018 *Dynamical Zeta Functions* Springer Ergeb. 68) | FAIL | E | Frontier wild_swing S140 on the FIRST dynamical-spectral / transfer-operator measurement on χ_P, recommended next pick per S139 critique single-pick annotation. Chebyshev-Lobatto barycentric collocation on [0,1], (M_grid+1)-dim matrix; cylinder truncation n_max sweeping {100, 200, 400, 800}; 200 controls per ensemble at M=80, n=400; 5 baselines (B_naive=Bernoulli ρ, B_supp=cardinality-matched random subset of [2..n_max], B_par=parity-matched, B_cra=Cramér 1/log n, B_crao=Cramér+odd-parity most stringent); plus Rademacher controls for Liouville. **Pre-registered falsifiers**: F-A unweighted h=1 reproduces Mayer/GKW spectrum within 1e-3 (sanity); F-B B_crao closure within ±2σ (mode E); F-C ≥3σ deviation persisting under refinement (mode I); F-D closed-form polylog identity (A-grade). **Outcome: B-grade case (i) mode E — F-A passes, F-B holds, F-C/F-D fail.** F-A: top-3 unweighted eigenvalues at M=160, n=800 = (0.99820, -0.30293, +0.10064) vs published GKW (1.0000, -0.30366300289, +0.10088637) — agreement < 0.2% on top-3, sanity ✓. **χ_P spectrum (M=120, n=400)**: |λ_0|=0.35961, |λ_1|=0.05103, |λ_2|=0.00792, |λ_3|=0.00128, |λ_4|=0.00021 — alternating signs, geometric decay (gap |λ_1|/|λ_0|=0.142), parallel to GKW structure. **Refinement-stable**: top-5 eigenvalues vary by <1% CV across (M_grid, n_max) ∈ {30..160}×{100..800}, real Pollicott-Ruelle resonances (not spurious-discretisation). **Closed-form prediction**: leading eigenvector of L_h overlaps unweighted Gauss density g(x)=1/((1+x)log2) at cosine ≥0.9919 for h ∈ {χ_P, λ, Λ}; arithmetic content lives in eigenvalue, not eigenfunction. Rayleigh-quotient closed form λ_0^h ≈ Σ_n h(n)·a_n with a_n=T_n/||g||², T_n=∫₀¹dx/[(1+x)(x+n)(x+n+1)·log²2] explicit (partial fractions); for n≥2: T_n=(1/log²2)·[ln2/(n(n-1)) - ln((n+1)/n)/(n-1) + ln((n+2)/(n+1))/n], asymptotic a_n~2log2/n², Σa_n=1. **At n_max=400: Σ_p a_p = 0.36187 vs measured |λ_0|^{χ_P}=0.35961, +0.6% rel error; Σ_n Λ(n)·a_n = 0.5206 vs measured |λ_0|^Λ=0.4968, +4.8% rel error.** For signed λ Rayleigh fails by factor 2 (left eigenvector ≠ const for signed weights). **Cramér closure**: vs B_crao 200 seeds at M=80, n=400: |λ_0| z=-1.79, |λ_1| z=-1.60, gap z=+2.10 — all within ±2σ Bonferroni (3-feature ≈ 2.39σ); F-B holds. vs B_supp z=+3.58 (uniform random over-spreads to high indices, false depression); vs B_par z=+2.02 (parity matched but lacks log-density profile). **A-grade hypothesis falsified**: |λ_0|/(π(N)/N) = 0.3596/0.195 = 1.844 — NOT a closed-form constant; the closed form IS Σ_p a_p, which is cost-equivalent to enumerating primes ≤ N. **Mechanism for closure mode E**: arithmetic-weighted PR leading resonance is a DENSITY-ONLY invariant at first moment — captured exactly by Σ_n h(n)·a_n where {a_n} is independent of h. Cramér model 1/log n + odd parity reproduces this expectation within sample noise. **Mayer-style dynamical-determinant representation of π(x) NOT opened**: there is no isolated arithmetic resonance distinct from the density-Rayleigh prediction. **Connections**: STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT from E2.13 Gowers (S85 detected HL → W-trick erases), E2.14 Anderson Lyapunov (S88 detected HL → W-trick erases), E2.16 anti-DPP (S95), E2.17 PH (S96), E2.19 subword (S104), E2.20 Mahler (S134), E2.21 Newman (S138) — all DETECT HL deviations; E2.22 (this work) shows the dynamical-spectral leading resonance is a 1-summing density integral that the Cramér model captures by construction. STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT from CLOSED line 320, 425 (unweighted ergodic-theory: orbit count = ζ-zeros, Furstenberg-correspondence circular) — D30 is the spectral theory of a SPECIFIC arithmetic-weighted operator, not the abstract correspondence. STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT from CLOSED line 105 (constructive transfer-matrix sieve: state space lcm(primes ≤√x) exponentially large) — D30 is spectral, not constructive. STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT from CLOSED line 182 (FRACTRAN automaton). **Adds EDGE E2.22**: λ_0^{χ_P} = Σ_p a_p (1+O(N⁻¹)) with a_n explicit closed form, reduces to Cramér 1/log n + odd parity within ±2σ at 200 seeds. EVS L (closed form exists, but not polylog-evaluable). **Cross-domain ingredient (Pollicott-Ruelle / transfer-operator spectral theory) performed real work**: first project quantitative use; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §5 row "Transfer operator spectrum (Pollicott-Ruelle resonances of arithmetic dynamical system)" promoted PROPOSED (D30) → USED E with edge E2.22. **Channelled mathematician**: Ruelle / Baladi (transfer-operator spectral theory) with Mayer 1991 (arithmetic transfer operators). **Successors proposed (S140)**: D30.a dynamical determinant det(I − z·L_{χ_P}) zero structure — does it factor like Mayer's `det(I − L_s) = ζ(2s)/etc.` for χ_P-weighted family? D30.b Mayer-style s-parameterised χ_P family L_{χ_P, s} f(z) = Σ_p (1/(z+p))^{2s} f(1/(z+p)); zeros of det(I − L_{χ_P, s}) define a NEW arithmetic Dirichlet-series-like function (2 sessions); D30.c Bowen-Series / doubling-map / β-shift analogues — does Cramér-typical χ_P leading-resonance persist on every hyperbolic dynamical system, or is it Gauss-specific (1 session). Cites E2.13, E2.14, E2.16, E2.17, E2.18, E2.19, E2.20, E2.21, E7.16 (Friedman-typical-once-support-and-parity-matched). Cross-domain refs: Pollicott 1985 *Inventiones* 81, 413; Ruelle 1976 *Inventiones* 34, 231; Mayer 1991 *Bull. AMS* 25, 55; Baladi 2018 *Dynamical Zeta Functions and Dynamical Determinants for Hyperbolic Maps* Springer Ergeb. 68; Liverani 2004 *Comm. Math. Phys.* 248; Wirsing 1974 *Acta Arith.* 24 (GKW constant). See `experiments/dynamical/pollicott_ruelle_chi_p/`, `archive/sessions/session140_d30_pollicott_ruelle_chi_p.md`. | 140 |
    | §L1 NOVELTY_CHALLENGES — Leading-row + dead-col upper-triangulation for E2.1 orthogonal corner `(W, d=j+1)`: which W ≤ 72 admit a Lean-tractable triangulation with rows ⊂ [0, W) and R = φ(W)+1 ≤ 22? | EXHAUSTED | C | Comprehensive enumeration via DP-based bitmask reachability on column subsets (script: `experiments/formalisations/E2_1_mps_bond_dim/leading_row_search.py`). For each W, build row signatures `S(r) ⊆ {chosen R cols}` as bitmasks; DP over T ⊆ R-cols to decide if some col ordering admits row assignment with first-σ-position ∈ A_i nonempty for every i. **Outcome**: leading-row family closes **EXACTLY** `W ∈ {2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 18, 20}` and is structurally obstructed for every other W in `[2, 72]` with `R ≤ 22`. Obstructed set: `{7, 9, 11, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 19, 21, 22, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 30, 32, 33, 34, 36, 38, 40, 42, 44, 48, 50, 54, 60, 66}`. **W=10 correction**: previously listed as obstructed in S128/S129 RESEARCH_AGENDA notes ("multiplicity-2 residue pattern"), the S144 DP found the missing triangulation `ρ ↦ (1, 0, 4, 3, 9)`, `σ ↦ (8, 1, 2, 0, 6)` (diagonal primes `{19, 2, 43, 31, 97}`), giving an eleventh unconditional `mps_bond_dim` instance. **Mechanism for exhaustion**: a leading-row triangulation of an `R × R` 0/1 sub-matrix with `R = φ(W) + 1` requires that for some col ordering `σ`, the row `bottom-row` (= row at position `R`) has exactly one prime-position in the chosen R cols. For W with `R ≥ 7` and composite structure {prime power × small prime}, no such "isolated diagonal hit" exists in rows `[0, W)` because chiP windows of length W cluster primes by residue class. The DP is sufficient (true negation): `reach[2^R - 1] = false` ⇔ no triangulation exists. **Closes**: routes A^{(N)} for N ≥ 12 are ruled out at this parameter range; further single-session corner closures must use either (a) `Matrix.det_of_blockTriangular` for non-triangulizable matrices (multi-session), or (b) cofactor-expansion-based determinant proofs (e.g., the W=9 7×7 sub-matrix has det = 1 by row cofactor expansion but no row+col upper-triangulation; ~250 Lean lines per such corner). **Adds EDGE (negative shape, in EDGES.md as a refinement of E2.1)**: leading-row + dead-col triangulation closes E2.1 orthogonal corner exactly for `W ∈ {2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 18, 20}` at `R ≤ 22`. **Cross-domain ingredient**: bitmask DP over reachability (algorithmic, not number-theoretic). **Channelled mathematician**: none — purely combinatorial enumeration. Cites E2.1, references S98/S99/S106/S107/S117/S122/S128/S129/S137/S143/S144 (the closure chain). See `experiments/formalisations/E2_1_mps_bond_dim/leading_row_search.py` and `experiments/formalisations/E2_1_mps_bond_dim/leading_row_search_results.txt`, `archive/sessions/session144_l1_lean_w10_corner_and_search.md`. | 144 |
    | §D5 ATTACK_VECTORS — Continuous-time quantum walk on the divisor / coprime graph: does U(t) = exp(−iH t) with seed `|1⟩` and target `v_p = (1/√π(x)) Σ_{p≤x} |p⟩` concentrate amplitude polylogarithmically on primes (Childs glued-tree analogue)? (cross-domain: Childs 2009 PRL 102 = arXiv:0806.1972 + Childs-Cleve-Deotto-Farhi-Gutmann-Spielman 2003 STOC = arXiv:quant-ph/0209131) | FAIL | E | Frontier wild_swing S141 on the natural CTQW successor to D4 (S80, Szegedy closed E). H_G ∈ {A, L = D − A} on G ∈ {D_x, C_x}. Eigendecomposition U(t) = Σ_k e^{−iλ_k t} u_k u_k^T; amplitude `P(t) = |⟨v_p|U(t)|1⟩|²` swept on t ∈ [0, 500] at 5001 grid points. **Pre-registered falsifiers**: F-A classical equilibration baseline `π(x)/x` (sanity); F-B peak amplitude beats Cramér + odd-parity matched control by a factor that grows with x (A-grade); F-C isolated band-edge eigenvector with O(1) overlap with v_p (glued-tree A-grade signature); F-D closure mode confirmed across both Hamiltonian variants. **Outcome**: F-A passes, F-B fails (excess +4–5σ but BOUNDED and not scaling), F-C fails (top overlap decays as `x^{−0.20}`, no isolated cluster), F-D confirms across all 4 variants. **Scaling (divisor + H=A, x ∈ {32, 64, 96, 128, 192, 256, 384, 512, 768}, T=500)**: ratio_max(x) := max_t P(t) / (π(x)/x) ∈ {1.24, 1.34, 1.39, 1.30, 1.23, 1.29, 1.31, 1.13, 1.22}; OLS fit `1.151 + 0.609/log(x)` → x→∞ asymptote ≈ **1.151** (bounded constant). top_overlap(x) := max_k |⟨u_k|v_p⟩| ∈ {0.626, 0.532, 0.469, 0.455, 0.420, 0.393, 0.369, 0.352, 0.328}; OLS log-log fit `1.214 · x^{−0.200}` (slow polynomial decay, NOT constant). **C2 control (Cramér + odd parity, 100 seeds)**: z_peak = +5.4, +3.3, +5.1 at x = 64, 128, 256; z_mean = +4.6, +4.1, +4.0 — modest persistent excess (~+4σ residual) but does NOT scale. C1 (uniform random subset) z = +2.9, +3.9, +7.5, +10.2 grows but smaller than C3. C3 (random ER graph, same mean degree) z = +10.6, +22.1, +39.9, +65.8 — divisor graph IS structured, but structure is positional (vertex-1 hub), not prime-detection. **Hamiltonian / graph variants (F-D)**: divisor + H=L (Laplacian): ratio_max → 0 as x grows (zero-mode of L kills coupling, |1⟩ has nontrivial overlap with all-ones eigenvector orthogonal to centred v_p); coprime + H=A: ratio_max DECREASES as `x^{−1/2}` (primes anti-concentrated relative to equilibrium); coprime + H=L: ratio_max → 0. All 4 variants close mode E with no polylog opening. **Eigenvector inspection (x=128, divisor H=A, top-overlap k=1, λ_1=−6.03, overlap 0.455)**: dominant entries vertex 1 (−0.436), vertex 4 (+0.293), vertex 2 (+0.284), vertex 6 (+0.264), vertex 3 (+0.181) — "anti-hub vs first-shell" mode; mean |amp| ratio prime/composite = 1.42; the apparent overlap with v_p is positional artefact (primes 2, 3 are in the first shell of vertex 1) NOT primality detection. **Mechanism for closure mode E**: time-averaged amplitude `⟨P(t)⟩_t = Σ_k |⟨u_k|v_p⟩|² · |⟨u_k|1⟩|²` is dispersed across O(N) modes; no isolated band-edge cluster has constant overlap with v_p; peak amplitude over t is bounded by ~`max_k |⟨u_k|v_p⟩|² · |⟨u_k|1⟩|²` × (constructive interference factor O(1)), giving `max_t P(t) ≤ C · π(x)/x` with C ≈ 1.15 across x ∈ {32..768}. **Closure structurally distinct from D4 (Szegedy)**: D4 closed via discriminant-matrix spectral gap `δ = 1/poly(x)` blocking polylog mixing; D5 closes via eigenstate overlap dispersal, which is the *Childs spectral-density* closure language. The two arguments are independent — Szegedy could fail on gap while CTQW succeeds on band-edge cluster (as for glued trees), but here BOTH fail on the divisor / coprime graph families because the relevant spectral structure is dispersed. **Adds EDGE E7.20 (CTQW amplitude ceiling)**: for G ∈ {D_x, C_x}, `H ∈ {A_G, L_G}`, seed `|1⟩`: `max_t |⟨v_p|exp(−iHt)|1⟩|² ≤ C·π(x)/x` (C ≈ 1.151 asymptote on D_x H=A; → 0 on Laplacian / coprime H=A variants). Top eigenvector overlap with v_p decays as `x^{−0.20}`. EVS shape (forbids polylog primality extraction by CTQW on these graphs). **Distinct from**: CLOSED 105 (constructive transfer matrix), 182 (FRACTRAN), 474 (Goldreich-Levin Hadamard), and from E7.13 (Szegedy on same graphs, different mechanism). **Connection to E7.16 / E2.22**: time-averaged amplitude residual vs Cramér+odd-parity matched control is +4σ, same magnitude as E2.22's PR-resonance Cramér closure — primes are density-and-parity Cramér-typical at the time-averaged CTQW amplitude level. **Cross-domain ingredient (Childs continuous-time quantum walks) performed real work**: first project use of CTQW spectral-density framework (D4 Szegedy uses discriminant-matrix algebra, fundamentally different); CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §1 row "Continuous-time quantum walks (CTQW)" promoted PROPOSED (D5) → USED E with edge E7.20. **Channelled mathematician**: Childs (CTQW spectral-density framework). **Successors proposed (S141)**: D5.a CTQW on LPS Ramanujan Cayley graph `Cay(PSL_2(F_p), prime-indexed quaternion generators)` — non-abelian Cayley with explicit Ramanujan spectral gap; tests whether non-abelian Cayley admits isolated band-edge clusters that abelian divisor / coprime do not (composes with D28 PROPOSED, 1-2 sessions); D5.b multi-vertex seed `|v_s⟩ = (1/√k) Σ_{i ∈ S} |i⟩` for arithmetic-relevant S (powers of 2, smooth numbers, square-free integers) — does the right seed unlock polylog amplitude on primes? (1-session screening). Cites E7.13 (D4 Szegedy structural template), E7.16 (Friedman density+parity matching), E2.22 (PR resonance Cramér closure structurally analogous). Cross-domain refs: Childs 2009 *PRL* 102, 180501 = arXiv:0806.1972; Childs-Cleve-Deotto-Farhi-Gutmann-Spielman 2003 STOC = arXiv:quant-ph/0209131. See `experiments/quantum/ctqw_chi_p/` and `archive/sessions/session141_d5_ctqw_chi_p.md`. | 141 |
